domain CompactV2ObservationProvenance:
  description |
    Identify the independent implementation defect that removed committed event
    provenance between observer perception and the deep-psyche input packet.

problem P1:
  |
    Projected sensory phenomena reached TurnObservationPacket, but their
    sourceEventIds were empty on the normal composed path.

premise P2:
  |
    Event provenance can affect deterministic reaction receipts and focus
    salience. The retained evidence does not establish how often this path ran
    in production or whether the missing provenance changed dialogue quality.

evidence E1:
  |
    projectObserverPerception emitted an opaque percept ID derived by
    observerPerceptId. Git blame traces that implementation to commit 115e2e04
    dated 2026-08-04.

evidence E2:
  |
    buildTurnObservationPacket reconstructed a different literal
    percept.<side>.<evidenceId> value when looking up basisEventIds. Git blame
    traces that lookup to commit 348cb2f5 dated 2026-08-10.

evidence E3:
  |
    A network-zero composed-path preparation passed distinct projected
    counterpart and ambient phenomena into all three ordinary psyche requests,
    while every resulting sourceEventIds array remained empty before the fix.

evidence E4:
  |
    The source-link unit test manually supplied the literal percept ID expected
    by buildTurnObservationPacket instead of obtaining the frame from
    projectObserverPerception. It therefore passed without exercising the
    incompatible producer and consumer together.

evidence E5:
  |
    psyche-reaction-policy collects sourceEventIds into its receipt, and
    character-focus-policy adds evidence-density salience from the number of
    sourceEventIds. Both still receive the phenomenon text independently.

evidence E6:
  |
    Commit 73111aa exports observerPerceptId as the single derivation, reuses it
    in packet evidence lookup, updates affected fixtures, and adds a composed
    projection-to-packet regression. The full test suite, typecheck, build, and
    network-zero preparation passed after that correction.

decision D1 based_on P1, E1, E2, E3:
  |
    Root cause: the packet consumer independently reconstructed an obsolete
    percept-ID representation instead of using the projection producer's opaque
    ID derivation. The two deterministic representations could not match, so
    committed basis event IDs were dropped from projected sensory items.

decision D2 based_on P1, E1, E2:
  |
    Contributing cause: observerPerceptId was private to the projection module,
    while the cross-module relationship was represented only as an untyped
    string convention. This enabled the later consumer to duplicate the
    contract incorrectly.

decision D3 based_on P1, E3, E4:
  |
    Escape cause: the source-link test fabricated a frame using the consumer's
    expected literal ID and did not compose the real projection producer with
    packet construction. Unit, type, and build checks therefore exercised a
    self-consistent test double rather than the actual boundary.

decision D4 based_on P2, E3, E5:
  |
    Confirmed impact: projected sensory items lost committed event provenance,
    reducing receipt provenance and the evidence-density component of focus
    salience while leaving their phenomenon text available. Production
    prevalence and dialogue-quality impact remain unknown. This defect did not
    cause the earlier replay's absent phenomena because that replay supplied no
    sensory evidence at all.

decision D5 based_on D1, D2, E6:
  |
    Corrective action: use observerPerceptId as the single producer and consumer
    implementation for opaque percept IDs. The source-producing mismatch is
    corrected in commit 73111aa.

decision D6 based_on D3, E6:
  |
    Recurrence prevention: retain a composed regression that projects validated
    evidence and then builds the observer packet, asserting both the safe
    phenomenon and its exact committed event provenance.
